    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Over the past decade, several states and school districts across the United States have moved to reduce the use of suspensions to address student behavioral disruptions in schools. Yet, there is little causal evidence on the effectiveness of discipline reform in the context of elementary schools. This thesis adds to the growing literature by demonstrating the causal effects of the passage and implementation of discipline reform on suspension rates in California public elementary schools. Using comparative interrupted time series (CITS) and synthetic control difference-in-differences (SDID) estimation strategies,  I find that the passage of A.B. 420 results in a reduction of between 0.66 (CITS) and 1.25 (SDID) percentage points in the average suspension rates for the highest suspension rate schools in the state. I find that male and black students experience larger declines in suspension rates than the schools that serve them. These results suggest that the passage and successful implementation of discipline reform can be an effective avenue for addressing worrying rates of suspensions. I explore the likely factors contributing to A.B. 420’s success and propose further lines of inquiry to test these hypotheses. I also discuss considerations for policy-makers looking to implement similar discipline reform successfully.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
